    "result": "I've successfully fixed the tax rounding issue in the invoicing system. \n\n## Problem Summary\nThe issue was in the `round_half_up` function in `invoicing/money.py`. It was documented as implementing \"round half away from zero\" but was actually using Python's default `round()` function which implements \"banker's rounding\" (round half to even). This caused tax calculations like 5% of $2.50 (12.5 cents) to round down to 12 instead of up to 13.\n\n## Solution\nI updated the `round_half_up` function to properly implement \"round half away from zero\" behavior by:\n\n1. Replacing the implementation to explicitly handle the rounding logic\n2. For positive numbers: adding 0.5 and truncating (to round up on .5 cases)\n3. For negative numbers: subtracting 0.5 and truncating (to round down away from zero on .5 cases)\n\n## Verification\n- All 85 tests now pass (previously 3 were failing)\n- The specific failing test case `tax_for_line(250, \"reduced\")` now correctly returns 13 instead of 12\n- The fix handles both positive and negative numbers correctly\n- The behavior now matches the documented expectation of \"round half away from zero\"\n\nThe fix ensures accurate tax calculations where half-cent amounts are properly rounded away from zero, resolving the reported discrepancy.",
